      "observation": "The chapter defines hyperbolicity of the constant-coefficient system by this spectral condition."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; Section 1.1, equations (1.8) and (1.9)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0; q_t + f′(q)q_x = 0",
      "observation": "The conservation-law differential equation differentiates the composed flux in space; its quasilinear form uses the flux Jacobian."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 25–26; printed pages 3–4; sentence crossing the page boundary",
      "anchor": "In fact the linear problem (1.1) is a conservation law with the linear flux function f(q) = Aq.",
      "observation": "This is the selected assertion: the previously defined constant-coefficient linear system is a conservation law with matrix-linear flux."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; Section 1.1.2, opening sentence",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "The source explicitly qualifies derivation of the differential conservation law from the integral form by smoothness and subsequently notes that interesting solutions can be discontinuous."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "x and t are real spatial and temporal variables; q : R × R → R^m is the unknown field (printed page 1, equation (1.1) and its explanatory paragraph).",
      "m is the number of components of q, and A is a constant m × m real matrix (printed page 1, paragraph following equation (1.1)).",
      "The flux is f(v) = Av for a state v in R^m; v is analyst notation distinguishing the flux argument from the field q(x,t) (printed pages 3–4, sentence identifying the linear flux, together with equation (1.8))."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The referenced problem has the homogeneous constant-coefficient form q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0 in one space dimension (printed page 1, equation (1.1) and preceding sentence).",
      "A is constant and real, with dimensions matching the m-component field (printed page 1, paragraph following equation (1.1)).",
      "The differential equations are understood where the displayed partial derivatives exist; this is inherited differential-form context, not an explicitly quantified regularity hypothesis in the selected sentence (printed pages 1 and 3, equations (1.1) and (1.8); printed page 4, Section 1.1.2 smoothness qualification)."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The linear problem (1.1) is a conservation law of the form (1.8) with the linear flux f(v) = Av (printed pages 3–4, sentence crossing the page boundary).",
      "Under this flux substitution, q_t(x,t) + ∂_x(f(q(x,t))) = 0 specializes to q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0. This identity of differential expressions explicates the selected assertion; the source does not separately state it as a quantified equivalence theorem (printed page 1, equation (1.1); printed page 3, equation (1.8); printed page 4, opening sentence)."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "Subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives (printed page 1, paragraph following equation (1.1)).",
      "f is the flux function, and f′(q) is its Jacobian matrix in the quasilinear expression (printed page 3, equations (1.8)–(1.9) and adjacent text).",
      "Aq denotes matrix multiplication of the constant m × m matrix by the state vector; the zero in the system is the zero vector in R^m, as determined by the stated dimensions (printed page 1, equation (1.1) and explanatory paragraph; printed page 4, f(q) = Aq).",
      "Hyperbolicity of (1.1) means that A has real eigenvalues with m linearly independent corresponding eigenvectors (printed page 3, opening discussion)."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The chapter studies time-dependent, homogeneous first-order systems with one spatial variable in the displayed formulation (printed page 1, introduction to equation (1.1)).",
      "The surrounding section concerns hyperbolic conservation laws. The selected identification as a conservation law does not expressly impose an additional spectral hypothesis; distinguishing its algebraic content from the chapter's hyperbolic setting is an analyst interpretation supported by the separate hyperbolicity definition and the unqualified identification sentence (printed page 3, opening discussion and Section 1.1; printed page 4, opening sentence).",
      "The source distinguishes the differential formulation from an integral formulation and discusses discontinuous solutions separately; the selected sentence itself does not formulate a weak-solution claim (printed page 4, Sections 1.1.1–1.1.2)."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Constancy of the coefficient",
        "details": [
          "The matrix belongs to the constant-coefficient problem, so the spatial derivative of the flux composition has no derivative-of-A term (printed page 1, introduction to and explanation of equation (1.1); printed page 4, opening sentence)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Vector system and scalar specialization",
        "details": [
          "The selected assertion refers to the m-component system, not only scalar advection (printed page 1, equation (1.1) and q : R × R → R^m).",
          "The source explicitly includes m = 1 as the scalar case, where A reduces to a scalar; it gives no separate exception to the linear-flux identification for that case (printed page 1, paragraph beginning 'The simplest case'; printed page 4, opening sentence)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Flux composition and derivative",
        "details": [
          "The spatial derivative in (1.8) acts on x ↦ f(q(x,t)), with t fixed; it is not merely the flux evaluated at q_x (printed page 3, equation (1.8)).",
          "For f(v) = Av, the Jacobian is A, explaining the agreement of quasilinear form (1.9) with (1.1); this is an analyst derivation from the displayed formulas (printed pages 1, 3, and 4, equations (1.1), (1.9), and the opening linear-flux sentence)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Scope of the assertion",
